2026-08-24. The SIMD question, investigated ahead of Run 20 and settled
against this kernel, recorded here so the investigation is not redone.
The survey read the GHC checkout under ~/r/horde-ad/ghc at 10.1.20260803
(d415f38a75) and the 9.12.1 and 9.14.1 release notes off their tags.
What decides the matter is structural, not a version: GHC has no gather
or scatter primop at any width (the vector MachOps stop at
broadcast/insert/shuffle/arithmetic) and no automatic vectorization
anywhere, so the strided read side of the regime-3 fill cannot
vectorize at all -- N scalar loads into packDoubleX2#, which itself
lowers to an insert chain that StgToCmm marks with a TODO about poor
codegen. Only the contiguous store side could use a vector write, and
that is the cheap side, so the expected gain for the fallback is nil.
The capability picture, should a contiguous kernel ever want it: 128-bit
(DoubleX2#, FloatX4#) compiles on the plain NCG since 9.12 with no -m
flag, SSE2 being the x86-64 baseline; 9.14 added 128-bit integer ops,
shuffle without -mavx and SSE correctness fixes, and is the safer floor;
and#/or#/xor#, abs# and sqrt# are HEAD-only; 256-bit is LLVM-only in
every version including HEAD (insert/pack/shuffle answer "please use
-fllvm", moves want -mavx2, the testsuite runs all 256-bit tests
llvm_ways). The scalar-indexed "As" load/store family
(writeDoubleOffAddrAsDoubleX2# and kin) is the unaligned one and the
right one for arbitrary element offsets; the vector-indexed family is
naturally-aligned at the Cmm level. Using any of it is MagicHash code
against GHC.Exts with a scalar fallback under CPP, no runtime feature
detection needed at 128 bits. Where SIMD would pay in this stack is not
this fallback but the contiguous kernels -- ox-arrays' Strided.Arith
arithmetic, and the canonicalized contiguous-run copies if those ever
stop being memcpy -- and the recorded -fllvm probe (the winner's fill
at ~0.90 of its native self, 64-byte loop heads) remains the cheaper
whole-binary route to wider code, with its known comparability costs.

