2026-08-25. The run-file split. Four things learned in the doing, each
already a rule somewhere and none of them archaeology anybody would want in
an instruction.

The corpus wrote eleven tables into the live `runs/run19.md` and nothing
said so. `--in-place` had taken `--readme`, and every case drives the
installers by pointing that at a copy; when the installs moved to
`--run-doc` the flag went on being accepted and the install quietly found
the newest file in `runs/` instead. `check-scripts.py` ends by diffing `git
status --porcelain` against its start and did not see it either, the file
being untracked at both readings. What caught it was the `?` gate on a
published cell, three rows later, in an unrelated run of `--check-doc`.

`--audit` was green before and is green after, and 22 cases lost their bug
verdict in between. Replaying the README of the day was tried first, on the
five cases whose plants are README-only, and it does not reach them: an era
README carries era FIGURES, so a fixture anchored on a figure this run's
write-up put there will not build against it. The other seventeen carry a
flag no older reader accepts, or take a document they cannot be aimed at.
So the mechanism the file already had for this is narrower than its own
docstring implies -- it works where a fixture's anchor is a convention and
not where it is a number.

`.gitignore`'s `run[0-9]*` matched `runs/run19.md`, which is the one thing
carrying a run number that is now tracked. `git add` refused it and said
so, which is the good failure; the re-include below it is `!runs/run[0-9]*.md`
and works because `runs` is not itself matched -- `s` is no digit.

The check that was supposed to catch a paragraph left standing from the
previous run had been going quiet exactly when a session most wanted it: it
compared the working README against `git show HEAD:README.md`, so committing
the write-up turned it off, and it said `there is no previous run to hold it
to` and passed. Two files made it a diff between two paths and the git
dependency went with it. That is written up beside the check; what is here
is that nobody had noticed the silence, and the case that covers it
(`chapter-head-carries-a-previous-run`) had been passing all along by
renumbering a heading in a copy of the committed file, which is a state no
real write-up is ever in.

